their loved ones died in a setting that  was supposed to keep them safe  and for years they've been calling for  justice at chelmsford crown court today  they took an important step towards that  goal  their demonstrations outside followed by  a sentencing insight  that saw an nhs trust find one and a  half million pounds  for its failures to manage ligature  points involved in patients deaths  i'm left here bereaved i'm left here  angry and i'm left here with no justice  for my son if they'd listen to what  happened to my son  there's people that have died after him  that would still be alive  melanie leahy's son matthew is 20 when  he was found dead in his room at the  linden center in  an adult mental health center run by the  north essex partnership trust  he died from hanging in a room where  that shouldn't have been possible  lisa morris's son ben also died at the  linden center in 2008  another found dead after being placed in  its care  every time you think of a nice memory of  ben he's  tarnished with all this he's tarnished  with all the unanswered questions  he's um with not knowing the full truth  it's like a torture  the trust in question is now the essex  partnership university trust but in its  previous guise  11 of its patients died by hanging  between october 2004  and march 2015 a result the judge said  of its woefully inadequate risk  management  the court heard how each death was  investigated and each time  recommendations  made to reduce ligature risks but these  recommendations  were routinely ignored some fixtures  like door hinges and wardrobe handles  remained in place for up to nine years  after it was recommended they be removed  and in some cases the same  ligature point played a role in multiple  deaths  in a statement essex partnership  university trust apologized for the  failings and said it was fully committed  to ensuring that  every lesson is learned  for those still grieving though this  isn't where it ends they're now  demanding  a statutory public inquiry the whole  purpose of this journey is because i  want to know the truth as to how and why  my son died  and i still don't know it i need staff  interviewed under oath  so almost i go and the clear message to  the government is i'm not alone and  we're not  we're not going away more than 70  families share those demands  and they say they won't rest until they  are met however painful  the journey is ivor bennett sky news  chelmsford
 

